{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PHASE 1 STATUS — verified PASS so far (each: post-deletion re-fetch, missing_bodies=[], unmarked count unchanged):\nREB-341 83->32, REB-190 109->23, REB-427 110->28, REB-131 118->21, REB-2605 145->5 (phase-1 only; its 140 truncated echoes deliberately untouched), REB-289 171->33, REB-2480 297->21, REB-327 303->27, REB-425 346->28, REB-424 361->28, REB-2733 453->21, REB-155 546->81, REB-3067 771->31. REB-3271/REB-3251 no-ops.\n\nOperational finding worth recording: Jira Cloud soft-caps DELETE throughput for this account at roughly 2/s regardless of client concurrency (measured at 5, 10, 12 and 30 workers; per-call latency rises with issue size rather than returning 429). The run is therefore ~4h of wall time for ~30k deletions, not minutes.\n\nOne in-run defect found and fixed: the first request wrapper counted HTTP 429 against the same bounded transient-fault budget, so a sustained throttle exhausted it and aborted mid-REB-3067 with 'exhausted retries'. This was fail-safe (abort, not mis-deletion). 429 now has its own effectively unbounded budget with capped backoff, separate from the bounded 5xx/connection budget. The run was resumed from live state with no loss — every completed issue re-verified independently."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787256692814278001,"uuid":"57555f02-b780-4344-a5a0-89d32d4a3687"}